The DGS-1210-16 G1 only has an un-equiped serial port on the PCB named J6.
Use a [[docs:techref:hardware:port.serial#usb.to.rs232.ttl.converter.module|serial TTL]] adapter to connect.

{{media:dlink:dgs-1210:dgs-1210-16_g1_serial_port.jpg?400|DGS-1210-16 G1 serial port}}
{{:media:doc:hardware:serial:cp2104.jpg?400|CP2104 TTL}}

^ Serial connection parameters  | 3.3V TTL  | 115200 / 8N1  |
| Models tested:                |           |               |
| DGS-1210-16 G1                |           |               |
| DGS-1210-52 F3                |           |               |

D-Link DGS-1210-28 F1,F2,F3 serial is available in J6

<code>
     UART pinout 
     -----------
    
      [oooo]J3 [o]ooo|J6
        |       ^ ||`------ GND
        |       | |`------- RX
        |       | `-------- TX
        |       `---------- Vcc (3V3)
        |
        `------------------ J3 is power input connector nearby J6 UART
</code>

Notes about attaching serial adapters:
  * When using a common USB-to-serial adapter, like the blue one in the DGS-1210-52 F3 photographs on this page, do not attach the 3,3V pin; those adapters are powered from USB. Their 3,3V line is an output to power also the device attached to it from USB. If you attach it to the switch, the devices' 3,3V levels are probably slightly different, leading to an additional current heating up the components. On the other hand, if using a RS232 level shifter like the model on the other photo with the DE9 plug, this might have to be powered from the switch's side, needing also the 3,3V line. If this is the case, it won't work without it, so always try first with only Tx, Rx, GND attached.
  * Adapter manufacturers don't agree in their documentation from which "point of view" Rx and Tx are named. If you don't see correct text, first try swapping these. The parameters 115200 8N1 are correct for all DGS-1210 models tested so far, so changing those won't help.
  * Adapter manufacturers also don't agree on cable colours. While this is often true, never assume without verification black always being GND, or red always being 3.3V.
==== Serial mod ====
In order to use the serial console, a 4-pin header or a cable must be soldered onto the board.
===== DGS-1210-16 G1 =====
I wanted to share my serial mod. I can now connect to the serial console without having to open the device.
  - Soldered a pin header (2.54 mm) to the serial port on the main board (J6).
  - Used a cable tie to fix the serial TTL adapter (there are two holes below the adapter which I used to fix it). I used a CP2102 adapter and bent the pins so they were facing upward.
  - Connect the adapter with the header.
  - There is a black tape on the backside of the front bezel, which I removed and to my surprise there was already a cut out in the metal plate. Hence I only had to cut the plastic to fit the USB plug through.
  - Closed case and enjoyed my mod :)

{{{{:media:dlink:dgs-1210:dgs-1210_serialmod1.jpg?400|header on J6 and serial adapter}}
{{{{:media:dlink:dgs-1210:dgs-1210_serialmod2.jpg?400|header and adapter connected}}
{{{{:media:dlink:dgs-1210:dgs-1210_serialmod3.jpg?400|USB port from adapter after step 4}}
{{{{:media:dlink:dgs-1210:dgs-1210_serialmod4.jpg?400|connected serial port}}
===== DGS-1210-52 F3 =====
 Tools needed: "Phillips" type screwdrivers of sizes 1 and 2, soldering iron with small enough tip.
  - Disconnect all cables from the switch.
  - Open the case as described above.
  - Disconnect the internal power cable from the main board.
  - With the big screwdriver, remove the 9 silver couloured screws fixing the main board to the case.  They are located near the board's edge and between the black ethernet transformers and the RJ45 ports.
  - With the small screwdriver, remove the 4 black screws holding the heatsink in place.  The heatsink is screwed onto 4 bolts on the bottom of the case, the bolts going through holes in the PCB.  Do **not** pull the board out of the case using the heatsink, it is fixed with pink "heat-conducting glue tape pads" (don't know how they are "officially" called in english…).  Better leave it in place on the chips.  If not, you must make sure that everything remains clean while you work, and be sure to fix the heat sink in the correct position afterwards. 
  - Lift the board a bit vertically upwards until the holes in the PCB are above the bolt height.
  - Pull the board out of the case to the front side.
  - Now you can solder a 4-pin header onto the board.
I suggest to test the console before closing the case again:

The pin with the small triangular arrow pointing to it is 3,3V, Tx and Rx follow, the pin next to the board's edge is GND. The large arrow on the board is pointing from VCC in GND direction, as seen on the photographs.
  - Insert the board, connect the internal power cable, but do not yet insert any screws.
  - Attach a serial adapter to the switch and a PC/Laptop/…, set the parameters to 115200 8N1.
  - Power up the switch, but be careful to keep your fingers away from 110/230V parts.
If you don't see it booting up on the console, the order of the pins might be wrong, try swapping Rx and Tx.

If you want to install an adapter permanently, I recommend to use the hole for the anti-theft lock which is located near the serial pins for the cable. The modification of the DGS-1210-16 G1 with the USB plug, which is described above, won't work this way on the DGS-1210-52 F3, because there is no matching hole in the metal case, and the position of the 4 pins on the PCB differs.

